KW  - Reservoir fluids
KW  - Thermodynamic properties
AB  - Abstract:
In recent years, carbon capture, utilization, and storage (CCUS) technologies have gained significant attention in response to serious global warming. In petroleum engineering, injecting CO2 into underground reservoirs serves as an effective method for enhancing oil recovery (EOR) while achieving geological CO2 storage. Several key mechanisms involved in CO2-EOR and storage, such as oil viscosity reduction, oil swelling, interfacial tension reduction, and CO2 dissolution, are governed by its diffusion behavior in reservoir fluids. Given the growing number of studies on this subject, this work provides a comprehensive review of CO2 diffusion in reservoir fluids, covering diffusion-related technologies, measurement methods, behavior in different liquid systems, and numerical simulations. First, applications related to CO2 diffusion are summarized to highlight their practical importance. Second, methods for determining CO2 diffusion coefficients are categorized into direct and indirect approaches, with sub-methods introduced and discussed. Third, research progress on CO2 diffusion in different reservoir fluids is delineated based on fluid type and reservoir conditions. Fourth, numerical simulation studies are reviewed to illustrate CO2 diffusion performance in a visualized manner. Finally, based on the summarized developments, current challenges and potential future directions in this field are outlined. This review aims to offer a clear and structured reference for researchers seeking a systematic understanding of CO2 diffusion in reservoir fluids and to support further advances in this evolving area.
