KW  - Materiality
KW  - Dialogic accountability
KW  - Epistemic pluralism
AB  - Sustainability reporting has become a central instrument of global corporate governance, promising transparency, comparability, and enhanced accountability across markets and societies. Yet, despite its rapid diffusion, growing concerns persist about whether dominant sustainability reporting frameworks adequately capture the socio-ecological realities and moral priorities of the Global South. This paper advances a critical conceptual analysis of sustainability reporting by examining the ontological, epistemological, and normative assumptions embedded in contemporary standards, with particular attention to the distinctions between enterprise-value-oriented frameworks, such as the ISSB’s IFRS S1/S2, and impact-oriented approaches exemplified by the GRI Universal Standards. Drawing on critical accounting, dialogic accounting, and postcolonial and decolonial scholarship, the paper conceptualizes sustainability reporting as a discursive practice that both enables and constrains accountability. While global standards can engender disclosure diffusion and professionalization, they also privilege particular forms of value, knowledge, and evidence, often marginalizing local epistemes, relational ethics, and justice-oriented concerns. These tensions are shown to crystallize most clearly in materiality determination, where philosophical commitments are operationalized, and hierarchies of relevance are reproduced. In response, the paper introduces PASS (Plural Accountability for Sustainability in the South) as a heuristic conceptual reporting architecture rather than a prescriptive reporting framework. PASS integrates decolonial epistemology, contextual ontology, and transformative accountability to provide an analytical lens for interrogating sustainability reporting practices and imagining alternative configurations of plural accountability in emerging economy contexts. The paper contributes to sustainability accounting scholarship by reframing reporting as a contested, world-making practice and by highlighting the need for epistemically plural, dialogic, and context-sensitive approaches to sustainability disclosure.
