KW  - Named entity recognition
KW  - Diffusion model
KW  - Multi-feature extraction
KW  - Triple-channel attention mechanism
AB  - Named Entity Recognition (NER), an important task in Natural Language Processing (NLP), faces the complex scenario of coexisting flat and nested entities. This work aims to address the limitations of existing methods by introducing the diffusion-based multi-feature extraction and fusion method for NER (DMFNER), a framework designed to progressively refine entity boundaries and enhance contextual representation. Existing NER methods exhibit low accuracy in capturing the semantic relationships between entity boundaries and types when dealing with complex nested structures. They also struggle to capture rich contextual information, multi-level semantic relationships, and fail to fully utilize multi-channel information. To overcome these challenges, DMFNER implements a boundary denoising diffusion approach for NER, generating named entities from noisy spans. Meanwhile, the method combines the Multi-Feature Extraction and Fusion (MFEF) module with the Triple-Channel Attention Mechanism (TCAM) module. The MFEF module extracts semantic information from different levels and scales using various feature extraction methods and selectively fuses these features, thereby enhancing DMFNER’s understanding of complex entities and contexts. The TCAM module further enhances the accuracy of span representation by dynamically adjusting feature weights. Experiments on five flat and nested NER datasets—ACE2004, GENIA, CoNLL2003, MSRA, and WNUT17—show that the DMFNER achieves F1 scores of 88.66%, 81.82%, 93.16%, 94.70%, and 64.49% respectively. Compared to the strongest existing baseline models, the proposed method achieves an average performance improvement of 0.558 percentage points across these datasets, representing the best results among the compared approaches. These results suggest that DMFNER has strong potential to enhance downstream tasks such as relation extraction, question answering, and knowledge graph construction in practical NLP systems. Additionally, ablation studies and case analyses further confirm the contribution of each component of the model to the overall performance improvement. The source code of DMFNER is publicly available at: https://github.com/zhenbotang/DMFNER.
